# Specification - Phase 1: Bug Fixes & Stability

## Requirements

Implement high-priority bug fixes and stability improvements:

1. **#72 Fix URL Parsing Crash on Double Colon / Complex Titles**:
   - Tasks containing double colons (e.g. `Doug:: double-check re: Thursday meeting`) or colon-delimited text crash list views with `TypeError: URL constructor: http:Doug:: is not a valid URL`.
   - Ensure title linkification / URL detection safely validates URLs before calling URL constructors, properly isolating URLs with schema prefixes (`http://`, `https://`) and handling colon delimiters gracefully without throwing exceptions.

2. **#89 Handle 100% Completed Projects in Selector**:
   - In the project selector dropdown, place 100% completed projects at the end of the list separated by a clear visual divider / separator.
   - Add a setting / checkbox in the user settings to completely hide 100% completed projects from the project selector.

3. **#59 Theme Include & CLI Data Fetching Robustness**:
   - Address failures where Taskwarrior configurations with theme includes or output formatting disrupt JSON data fetching.
   - Ensure backend task queries enforce color suppression (`rc._forcecolor=off`, `rc.defaultwidth=0`) so theme directives and ANSI escape codes do not corrupt parsed task outputs.

4. **#90 Prevent Input Field Offset on Mobile (iOS)**:
   - Fix issue where mobile (iOS WebKit) virtual keyboard and input cursor become disconnected / offset after field validation errors.
   - Adjust input validation layout and container properties (e.g. `hide-details="auto"` or stable input slot dimensions) so viewport scroll re-calculation preserves correct cursor/focus alignment.

## User Stories
- As a user with task titles containing colons, double colons, or notes, I want the task list to render without crashing on URL parsing.
- As a user managing many completed projects, I want completed projects grouped below a divider in the project selector and the ability to hide them via a setting so the dropdown remains clean.
- As a user with themes or customized `.taskrc` configurations, I want the web interface to reliably fetch and parse task data without JSON decoding errors.
- As a mobile user on iOS, I want input fields to remain aligned with the cursor even after validation errors occur.
